WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Validation should happen before business logic depends on the data.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:14.040
This is the parse-then-use pattern: accept raw input, inspect it, convert it into a trusted internal object, and only then let application logic use it.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:18.460
If the input cannot be safely converted, reject it through a clear error path.

4
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.260
Parse-then-use is different from scattering small checks everywhere.

5
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:28.440
When validation is centralized near the trust boundary, the rest of the system can work with stronger assumptions.

6
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:39.980
Functions deeper in the application should not have to repeatedly ask whether a required identifier is present, whether a date is meaningful, or whether an enum-like value is one of the allowed cases.

7
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.340
Good validation checks both technical structure and business meaning.

8
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:52.120
Technical checks answer questions such as whether a value is an object, whether a field is a string, and whether an array length is within a safe range.

9
00:00:52.220 --> 00:01:02.220
Business checks answer questions such as whether a transition is allowed, whether a requested quantity is positive, or whether a value is valid for the current tenant, feature, or workflow.

10
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.700
Safe defaults can be useful, but silent correction can also hide bugs.

11
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.680
If a missing page size silently becomes a default, that may be fine.

12
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:19.020
If a malformed role silently becomes a low-privilege role, that might hide a caller defect that should be investigated.

13
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.000
Defensive design should distinguish intentional defaults from surprising repair.

14
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.760
Validation errors should be useful without revealing sensitive internals.

15
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:36.140
Operators and developers need enough detail to diagnose bad input, incompatible clients, or data drift.

16
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.920
External callers may need a stable, limited message.

17
00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:48.020
Avoid exposing secrets, stack traces, private payloads, or implementation details in places where they do not belong.

18
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.940
Validation is also a maintenance boundary.

19
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.240
When data contracts change, validators become a visible place to update expectations.

20
00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:04.740
That makes the system easier to reason about than code that accepts anything and discovers the problem much later, after partial work has already happened.
